# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Class 5 Ch 13 Animal Jumps: long-form tool descriptions (master prompt format), one per tool.
DESCRIPTIONS and OBJECTIVES are keyed by the tool media id."""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Hidden Number Box\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Exploration (the free first try of the chapter)\n"
        "- Textbook source: Chapter 13 Animal Jumps, the opening \"Find the hidden numbers\" box with 28, 36, 48 and 72 and its three questions. All other numbers are practice values (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student sees that a multiplier fits when every number that came out of the box divides by it exactly, and that more than one multiplier can fit.\n"
        "- Out of scope: the words factor and multiple (the video after this tool names them), arrays, common multiples, prime numbers.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A flat storybook stone clearing. A brown cardboard box (a cut-out like the book's, the largest object, a third of the stage wide) has a red arrow curving into its open top and another curving out. A round badge on its front reads \"×5\". On a wooden shelf at the left sit four round number stones: 2, 3, 4 and 7. Robby the rabbit and Deeku the deer (flat drawings) sit in the bottom corners.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"This box multiplies by 5. What will come out when 2 goes in?\"\n"
        "- Three answer tags (7, 10, 25) float above the box. The child taps one, then drags the stone 2 into the box.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Badge: shows the multiplier; a round minus and plus under it set 1 to 12 (greyed out in Activities 1 and 5, where it is fixed).\n"
        "- Out side: green wavy-edged number tags like the book's, each an eighth of the stage wide, in a row under the out arrow. From Activity 2 on they are there from the start.\n"
        "- Inside tray under the box: one dotted slot under each tag. Lever: a red curved-arrow lever on the box's right side, labelled \"Run backwards\". Keep shelf: a wooden shelf at the right edge for up to 6 round multiplier badges, empty at the start.\n"
        "- Goal chip at the top left of the stage, grey until the activity is done. No scores.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Forward: drag a stone into the open top. The box shakes and a tag flies out with a line such as \"2 × 5 = 10\".\n"
        "- Backward: set the badge, then pull the lever. The tags fly back in one by one, left to right. A number that divides exactly drops a whole number into its slot with a soft chime and the line \"10 ÷ 5 = 2\". One that does not bounces back out with \"15 ÷ 2 is not a whole number\".\n"
        "- Keep: drag the badge onto the shelf. It stays only if every tag dropped a whole number; otherwise it slides back with the line of the first tag that bounced. Drag a badge off to remove it.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (back to the activity's first screen) and one that reads \"Check\", then \"Next\" when the goal chip is green.\n"
        "- A guess is never marked wrong. If it differs from the result the strip says, for example, \"You guessed 7. The box multiplies, so 2 × 5 = 10.\"\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"This box multiplies by 5. What will come out when 2 goes in?\" Goal chip: \"Four numbers out\". Setup: badge fixed at 5; stones 2, 3, 4 and 7. Guess: tap 7, 10 or 25 for what 2 gives, then drag all four stones in. Result: tags 10, 15, 20 and 35 fly out. Why: \"2 × 5 = 10, 3 × 5 = 15, 4 × 5 = 20 and 7 × 5 = 35. Each came out 5 times as big.\" Help: guided, the line shows every time.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"10, 15, 20 and 35 came out. Which multipliers could the box have used?\" Goal chip: \"Keep the ones that fit\". Setup: tags 10, 15, 20, 35; cards 1, 2 and 5 glow beside the badge. Guess: tap the cards you think fit; then try each with the lever and keep the ones that fit. Result: 1 and 5 are kept. Why: \"10, 15, 20 and 35 all divide by 5 exactly: 2, 3, 4 and 7 went in. 15 ÷ 2 is not a whole number, so 2 does not fit.\" Help: guided, the three cards glow.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"16, 40 and 56 came out. Find every multiplier that fits.\" Goal chip: \"Find every multiplier\". Setup: tags 16, 40, 56; badge free 1 to 12. Guess: tap the biggest you think fits: 4, 8 or 12. Result: 1, 2, 4 and 8 sit on the shelf. Why: \"16 ÷ 8 = 2, 40 ÷ 8 = 5 and 56 ÷ 8 = 7. 8 is the biggest multiplier that fits.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 4 (independent). Goal line: \"21, 35 and 63 came out. Does 3 fit? Does 7 fit?\" Goal chip: \"Test 3 and 7\". Setup: tags 21, 35, 63. Guess: tap \"Yes\" or \"No\" for 3; then test 3 and 7 and keep the one that fits. Result: 7 is kept. Why: \"21 ÷ 7 = 3, 35 ÷ 7 = 5 and 63 ÷ 7 = 9. But 35 ÷ 3 is not a whole number: 11 × 3 = 33 and 12 × 3 = 36.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Activity 5 (independent). Goal line: \"The box multiplies by 6. What is the same about the numbers that come out?\" Goal chip: \"Spot what is the same\". Setup: badge fixed at 6; stones 2, 5, 7. Guess: before dropping, tap \"They all divide exactly by 6\", \"They are all odd\" or \"They all end in 2\". Then drop the stones: tags 12, 30, 42 fly out. Result: the first line turns green (if the guess was another, the child taps again). Why: \"12 ÷ 6 = 2, 30 ÷ 6 = 5 and 42 ÷ 6 = 7. Every number that comes out divides exactly by 6.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line (the book's question): \"Can you guess the multiplier if you see the 4 numbers coming out of the box?\" Goal chip: \"Find a multiplier\". Setup: tags 28, 36, 48, 72; badge free. Guess: the child sets the multiplier they believe in, then pulls the lever. Result: any of 1, 2 and 4 is accepted; for 4 the tray shows 7, 9, 12 and 18. Why: \"28 ÷ 4 = 7, 36 ÷ 4 = 9, 48 ÷ 4 = 12 and 72 ÷ 4 = 18. All four are whole numbers, so 4 fits.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line (the book's questions): \"Is there more than one possible multiplier? What numbers might have been put inside the box?\" Goal chip: \"Keep every multiplier that fits\". Setup: the same four tags. Guess: tap \"Yes\" or \"No\" for \"more than one multiplier?\". The child tries the badge from 1 to 12, keeps 1, 2 and 4, then taps each kept badge to see what went inside: 4 gives 7, 9, 12, 18; 2 gives 14, 18, 24, 36; 1 gives 28, 36, 48, 72. Why: \"1, 2 and 4 all fit, so the box could have used more than one multiplier.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Forward the box multiplies, so backwards it divides. The child sees that a multiplier fits only if every number that came out divides by it exactly: 3 fits 36, 48 and 72 but not 28, so it is not the box's multiplier. The same numbers can come from more than one multiplier, and 1 always fits.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"Divide each number that came out. If every answer is a whole number, that multiplier fits.\" \"More than one multiplier can fit.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick, Robby does a small hop. Confetti only on the end screen.\n"
        "- Wrong: a red strip, a small shake of the object that caused it and one friendly line. Retry is always free.\n"
        "- Mistake: keeps a multiplier that does not fit, for example 3 for 28, 36, 48, 72 -> \"28 ÷ 3 is not a whole number, so 3 cannot be the multiplier. Look at the 28 tag.\" The 28 tag pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: thinks a multiplier that fits some tags is enough (3 fits 36, 48 and 72) -> \"3 fits three tags but 28 bounced. The box used one multiplier for all four numbers.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: stops after the biggest multiplier -> on Check: \"There are 2 more multipliers that fit. Try smaller ones.\" The shelf pulses; the tool counts what is missing.\n"
        "- Mistake: Activity 5 line \"They are all odd\" -> \"12 came out, and 12 is not odd. Divide each tag by 6 and look.\"\n"
        "- Hints, 3 levels: (1) words: \"The box multiplied each number. Going back, divide. Does each number that came out divide exactly?\" (2) visual: the badge glows on the first multiplier worth trying (Activity 2: 5; Activity 3: 2; Activity 4: 7; star challenges: 2) and the lever pulses; in Activities 1 and 5 the first stone glows. (3) almost the answer: the tool runs it: \"Set the badge to 4: 28 ÷ 4 = 7, 36 ÷ 4 = 9, 48 ÷ 4 = 12, 72 ÷ 4 = 18. All four are whole, so 4 fits. Now try a smaller one.\" Activity 1: \"2 × 5 = 10. Now drop 3.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: multiplier 1 -> every tag drops back unchanged: \"With 1 the box changes nothing: the numbers that went in are the numbers that came out.\" It always fits. A multiplier that fits only one tag, such as 7 for 28, shows the first tag that bounced.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1 is done when a multiplier that fits (1, 2 or 4) is run. Star Challenge 2 is done when 1, 2 and 4 are on the shelf and each kept badge has been tapped once.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"Divide each number that came out. If every answer is a whole number, that multiplier fits.\" \"More than one multiplier can fit, and 1 always fits.\" \"Going backwards shows what could have gone in.\" Think and talk: \"Could the box's multiplier be bigger than the smallest number that came out? Why or why not?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: stone clearing background (flat drawing); open cardboard box with a blank front; red curved arrows; green wavy-edged tags and round number stones (code-drawn); wooden shelf and tray; Robby the rabbit and Deeku the deer (flat drawings). Language: English."
    ),
    "M1.S4.T4.C10.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Array Maker\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M1.S4.T4.C10.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Chapter 13 Animal Jumps, the array of 15 tiles, the four blanks of 12, the Let Us Do on arrays (10, 14, 13, 20, 25, 32, 37, 46, 54) and the prime numbers note (13 and 37). Practice values: 8, 9, 11 (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student sees that each way of laying a number of tiles in equal rows is a multiplication whose two numbers are factors of the number, and that a number with only the array 1 × the number has just two factors.\n"
        "- Out of scope: common factors of two numbers, tests for divisibility, finding prime numbers by any method other than arrays.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A pale wooden tile mat (photo texture) on a stone terrace. At its left an open cloth bag has spilled a pile of 8 flat blue square tiles, each a thumb-width. A rows stepper (round minus, \"rows\" with a big number, round plus) at the mat's left edge reads 1. An empty wooden shelf along the bottom will hold the factors.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Make every array of 8 tiles.\"\n"
        "- Beside the pile a prompt \"Will 3 rows share them equally?\" has two big buttons, \"Yes\" and \"No\". The child taps one, then presses the plus on the stepper.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Mat: faint grid, room for 12 rows. Tiles are dealt like cards, one to each row in turn from the top; the tile size shrinks so any array fits.\n"
        "- Equal rows: every row ends at the same place and the array glows green, and a card appears under the mat, for example \"2 × 4 = 8\". Unequal rows: the right edge is ragged and the last tile of each longer row is tinted.\n"
        "- Factor shelf: round wooden discs appear on it in order of size; a counter reads \"Factors found: 0\".\n"
        "- Rows-tried strip: numbered dots 1 to 12 under the stepper; a dot is green if its rows were equal, grey if not.\n"
        "- Turn round: a round-arrow button at the mat's top right. Goal chip at the top left. Each saved array has its own colour: blue, green, purple, pink, as in the book.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Plus and minus deal the tiles into 1 up to the smaller of 12 and the number of tiles.\n"
        "- Save: tap the card; it flies to the shelf and its two numbers become discs (3 × 3 gives one).\n"
        "- Turn round swaps rows and tiles in a row: 2 rows of 4 become 4 rows of 2 and the strip says \"4 × 2 is the 2 × 4 array turned round. It gives no new factor.\"\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (clears the mat and shelf) and one that reads \"Check\", then \"Next\" when the goal chip is green.\n"
        "- A guess is never marked wrong. If it differs from the result the strip says, for example, \"You guessed No. Look: 3 rows make rows of 3, 3 and 2.\"\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Make every array of 8 tiles.\" Goal chip: \"Find the arrays of 8\". Setup: 8 tiles. Guess: \"Yes\" or \"No\" for 3 rows. The child tries 1, 2, 3 and 4 rows and saves each equal array. Result: 3 rows are unequal; 1, 2 and 4 rows are equal. Why: \"1 × 8 = 8, 2 × 4 = 8 and 4 × 2 = 8, so the factors of 8 are 1, 2, 4 and 8.\" Help: guided, the rows-tried strip is on.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Make every array of 9 tiles.\" Goal chip: \"Find the arrays of 9\". Setup: 9 tiles. Guess: tap how many factors 9 will have: 2, 3 or 4. The child saves 1 × 9 and 3 × 3. Result: the shelf shows 1, 3 and 9. Why: \"3 rows of 3 is a square, so 3 shows only once. The factors of 9 are 1, 3 and 9.\" Help: guided, the strip is on.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Make every array of 11 tiles.\" Goal chip: \"Try every number of rows\". Setup: 11 tiles. Guess: tap how many arrays 11 will make: 1, 2 or 3. The child tries rows 2 to 11. Result: only 1 row and 11 rows are equal; the shelf shows 1 and 11. Why: \"Only 1 × 11 fills equal rows, so 11 has only two factors: 1 and 11.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line (the book's question): \"A number, when arranged in an array, shows the factors of that number. Are there other numbers that are factors of 15? Try to make other arrays for the number 15.\" Goal chip: \"Other arrays of 15\". Setup: 15 tiles in 3 rows of 5 as in the book, card \"3 × 5 = 15\", \"Factors\" under the 3 and 5, \"Multiple\" under the 15; the shelf holds 3 and 5. Guess: \"Yes\" or \"No\" for other factors. The child saves 1 × 15. Result: the shelf shows 1, 3, 5 and 15. Why: \"1 × 15 = 15 and 3 × 5 = 15. The factors of 15 are 1, 3, 5 and 15.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line (the book's question): \"Do you see why 12 is a multiple of 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, and 12?\" with the blanks \"2 × ___ = 12\", \"3 × ___ = 12\", \"12 × ___ = 12\", \"1 × ___ = 12\". Goal chip: \"Fill the 4 blanks\". Setup: 12 tiles; a rack of tiles numbered 1 to 12. Guess: the child drags a numbered tile into a blank; the mat then deals 12 tiles into the first number of rows and shows one row. Result: 6, 4, 1 and 12 turn the blanks green. Why: \"2 × 6 = 12, 3 × 4 = 12, 12 × 1 = 12 and 1 × 12 = 12. A factor times a whole number makes 12, so 12 is a multiple of each of them.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line (the book's question): \"Make different arrays for the following numbers. Identify the factors in each case.\" Goal chip: \"Numbers (a) to (i)\". Setup: cards (a) 10, (b) 14, (c) 13, (d) 20, (e) 25, (f) 32, (g) 37, (h) 46, (i) 54 above the mat with nine dots for progress. Per number: tap a button 2 to 8 to guess the number of factors, deal rows, save every equal array, press \"Check\". Result: (a) 1, 2, 5, 10; (b) 1, 2, 7, 14; (c) 1, 13; (d) 1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 20; (e) 1, 5, 25; (f) 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32; (g) 1, 37; (h) 1, 2, 23, 46; (i) 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18, 27, 54 (saved from 1 × 54, 2 × 27, 3 × 18 and 6 × 9). Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line (the book's question): \"Numbers like 13 and 37 are called prime numbers. Why?\" Goal chip: \"Why prime?\". Setup: the nine cards with factor counts hidden. Guess: tap the cards you think have only two factors. Then the counts slide in (4, 4, 2, 6, 3, 6, 2, 4, 8) and the child picks one of three lines: \"Each has only one array, 1 × the number, so only two factors.\", \"They are odd numbers.\" or \"They are big numbers.\" Result: the first line turns green. Why: \"13 and 37 make only 1 × 13 and 1 × 37, so each has only two factors. Such numbers are called prime numbers.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- Dealing tiles shows that only some row counts make equal rows. Each equal array is a multiplication whose two numbers are factors; turning it round gives the same two numbers. The child always finds 1 and the number itself, and sees that 11, 13 and 37 have no other array.\n"
        "- Rules in the child's words: \"The two numbers in every array are factors.\" \"1 and the number itself are always factors.\" \"Only one array means only two factors: a prime number.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the array glows green, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick. Confetti only on the end screen.\n"
        "- Wrong: a red strip, a small shake and one friendly line. Retry is free.\n"
        "- Mistake: unequal rows saved -> \"8 tiles in 3 rows make rows of 3, 3 and 2. The rows are not equal, so 3 is not a factor of 8.\" The short rows pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: leaves out 1 and the number itself -> on Check: \"1 row of all the tiles is an array too: 1 × 8 = 8.\" The stepper pulses on 1.\n"
        "- Mistake: says 13 has no factors -> \"13 has two factors: 1 row of 13 tiles gives 1 × 13.\" The 1 and 13 discs pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: stops early -> on Check: \"You have 3 factors. There is 1 more to find.\" The next untried dot pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong tile in a blank of Star Challenge 2 -> \"2 rows of 5 tiles hold 10 tiles, not 12.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: Star Challenge 4 line \"They are odd numbers\" -> \"25 is odd too, but 5 rows of 5 is another array.\" Line \"They are big numbers\" -> \"54 is bigger than 37 and makes four arrays.\"\n"
        "- Hints, 3 levels: (1) words: \"An array needs every row to be the same length. Try 1 row first, then 2 rows, then 3.\" (2) visual: the stepper and the rows-tried strip pulse on the next row count not yet tried. (3) almost the answer: the tool deals the next missing array and shows its card unsaved, for example for 15 \"1 row of 15 tiles is equal: 1 × 15 = 15. Tap the card to save it.\" In Star Challenge 2: \"Count the tiles in one row.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: a square such as 9 or 25 saves one disc. 1 row is always equal. For 13 and 37 every row count from 2 to 12 is unequal.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Each star challenge is done when its result above is on the screen: the shelf with 1, 3, 5 and 15; four green blanks; nine green dots; 13 and 37 tapped with the first line chosen.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"The two numbers in every array are factors.\" \"1 and the number itself are always factors.\" \"Only one array means only two factors: a prime number.\" Think and talk: \"Why does 25 have only 3 factors when 10 has 4? Look at their arrays.\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: pale wooden tile mat (photo texture); stone terrace (flat drawing); open cloth bag; coloured square tiles and round number tiles 1 to 12 (code-drawn); wooden shelf with round discs. Language: English."
    ),
    "M2.S7.T7.C21.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Jump Together\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S7.T7.C21.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Chapter 13 Animal Jumps, the rabbit and frog number line, the lists of multiples of 4 and 6, Let Us Do questions 1 and 2. Practice pairs 2 and 7, 6 and 10, 4 and 5 (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student finds the stones that two jumpers both land on, calls them common multiples, and sees that they are the multiples of the first one.\n"
        "- Out of scope: common factors, three or more jumpers, any way of finding common multiples except jumping and lists.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A row of grey river stones across a jungle stream (photo-style, numbered 0 to 72 in big paint, in three rows: 0 to 24, 25 to 48, 49 to 72). Robby the rabbit (teal ring mark) and Deeku the deer (amber diamond mark), flat drawings, stand on stone 0. Under each a jump dial shows its size: Robby 2, Deeku 7 (locked here).\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Robby jumps 2 and Deeku jumps 7. Which stones do both land on?\"\n"
        "- Three tags (9, 14, 28) float above the stones. The child taps the first stone they think both will land on, then presses \"Run\".\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Stones: a thumb-width each. A stone an animal lands on gets its mark; a stone both land on glows gold with both marks.\n"
        "- Dials: under each animal a minus, the size, a plus (2 to 12) and a round hop button. A \"Run\" button between them makes both hop to the end.\n"
        "- Tray under the stones, \"Both land on\": gold numbers drop into it in order. Lists: a book icon at the top right shows the multiples of each size up to 72 as two rows of chips, numbers in both rows ringed gold.\n"
        "- Road (Star Challenge 4 only): 65 cobbled stones numbered 0 to 64 winding in four rows, as in the book, with the food (a carrot and mushrooms) after 64.\n"
        "- Goal chip at the top left of the stage.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Set a size with minus and plus (both animals may have the same size). Tap a hop button for one jump or \"Run\" for all. Tap a stone to guess it (a white ring); tap a gold stone to mark the first common multiple.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3 adds a gold token beside the tray: each tap adds the next common multiple after the last one in the tray, even past stone 72.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (animals back on 0) and one that reads \"Check\", then \"Next\" when the goal chip is green.\n"
        "- A guess is never marked wrong. If it differs from the result the strip says, for example, \"You guessed 9. Let us see where they meet.\"\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Robby jumps 2 and Deeku jumps 7. Which stones do both land on?\" Goal chip: \"Find the gold stones\". Setup: dials locked at 2 and 7. Guess: tap 9, 14 or 28 for the first stone both land on, then \"Run\". Result: stones 14, 28, 42, 56 and 70 turn gold. Why: \"Both land on 14, 28, 42, 56 and 70. 2 × 7 = 14, so Robby needs 7 jumps and Deeku 2.\" Help: guided, each landing shows its sum, for example \"Robby 2 × 7 = 14\".\n"
        "- Activity 2 (guided). Goal line: \"Robby jumps 6 and Deeku jumps 10. Which is the first stone they both land on?\" Goal chip: \"First common multiple\". Setup: dials locked at 6 and 10; Lists on. Guess: tap 16, 30 or 60; then \"Run\" and tap the first gold stone. Result: stones 30 and 60 turn gold. Why: \"6 × 5 = 30 and 10 × 3 = 30, so 30 is the first stone both land on. 6 × 10 = 60 is gold too, but it is not the first.\" Help: guided.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Robby jumps 4 and Deeku jumps 5. They met at 20 and 40. Where is the next gold stone?\" Goal chip: \"Find the next gold stone\". Setup: dials locked at 4 and 5; 20 and 40 are already gold. Guess: tap the stone you think comes next, then \"Run\". Result: 60 turns gold. Why: \"20, 40 and 60: each is 20 more than the one before. 4 × 5 = 20, and the next one, 80, is past the end.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line (the book's question): \"12 is the first common multiple of 3 and 4. What are some other common multiples of 3 and 4? You can continue the number line or take help from the times tables of 3 and 4.\" Goal chip: \"Other common multiples\". Setup: a rabbit (4) and a frog (3), dials locked; stone 12 is gold, boxed. Guess: tap at least 3 stones you think are also gold, then \"Run\". Result: 24, 36, 48, 60 and 72 turn gold; wrong taps show why. Why: \"24, 36, 48, 60 and 72 are common multiples of 3 and 4. Each is 12 more than the one before.\" Help: independent; the book icon shows the times tables.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line (the book's question): \"Let us write the multiples of two numbers, 4 and 6. Multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12, 16, ... Multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18, ... 12 and 24 are two of the common multiples of 4 and 6. List a few more.\" Goal chip: \"List a few more\". Setup: Lists on; 12 and 24 ringed. Guess: the row for 4 shows first; tap numbers you think are also multiples of 6, then the row for 6 slides in. Result: rings on 12, 24, 36, 48, 60 and 72; 2 new correct numbers finish it. Why: \"36 = 4 × 9 and 36 = 6 × 6, so 36 is in both rows.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line (the book's question): \"Find 5 common multiples of the following pairs of numbers.\" Goal chip: \"Pairs (a) to (i)\". Setup: nine pairs, one at a time, dials locked. Guess: three tags for the first common multiple: (a) 2 and 3: 5, 6, 12; (b) 5 and 8: 13, 40, 80; (c) 2 and 4: 4, 6, 8; (d) 3 and 9: 9, 12, 27; (e) 5 and 10: 10, 15, 50; (f) 9 and 12: 21, 36, 108; (g) 8 and 12: 20, 24, 96; (h) 6 and 8: 14, 24, 48; (i) 6 and 9: 15, 18, 54. Then \"Run\". The child fills five tray slots by tapping gold stones or the gold token. Result: the first common multiples are (a) 6, (b) 40, (c) 4, (d) 9, (e) 10, (f) 36, (g) 24, (h) 24, (i) 18; the sample five are that number times 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. Why: \"Any 5 common multiples are right. Your answer may differ.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line (the book's question): \"Food is available at the end of a cobbled road. Robby, the rabbit, takes a jump of 4 each time. Deeku, the deer, takes a jump of 6 each time. They both start at 0. Will both Robby and Deeku reach the food? Who will reach first? How do you know? Explain your answer.\" Goal chip: \"Who reaches the food?\". Setup: the cobbled road, dials locked at 4 and 6. Guess: tap \"Robby\", \"Deeku\" or \"Both\" for who reaches the food, then \"Run\". Result: Robby lands on 64; Deeku lands on 60 and then 66; stones 12, 24, 36, 48 and 60 are gold. The child taps \"Robby is first\" and the reason \"64 is a multiple of 4 but not of 6\". Why: \"4 × 16 = 64, but 6 × 10 = 60 and 6 × 11 = 66, so Deeku never lands on 64. Only Robby reaches the food, so he is first.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- An animal lands only on the multiples of its size, so a stone both land on is a common multiple. The gold stones are equally far apart: they are the multiples of the first one.\n"
        "- The first common multiple is not always the product (30, not 60, for 6 and 10).\n"
        "- Rules: \"A stone both animals land on is a common multiple.\" \"The common multiples are the multiples of the first one.\" \"The list never ends.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the stone glows gold, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick. Confetti only on the end screen.\n"
        "- Wrong: a red strip, a small shake and one friendly line. Retry is free.\n"
        "- Mistake: taps a stone only one animal lands on -> \"Only Robby lands on 8. Deeku jumps from 7 to 14 and skips it.\" The stone pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: says the first common multiple is the product, for example 60 for 6 and 10 -> \"6 × 10 = 60 is a common multiple, but a smaller stone is gold too. Look for the first gold stone.\" That stone pulses.\n"
        "- Mistake: stops at the first gold stone -> \"The animals keep jumping, so there are more gold stones after 12.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: gives a jump size, for example 2 for 6 and 10 -> \"2 goes into 6 and 10, but we want a stone both land on.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: Star Challenge 4, says both reach the food -> \"Deeku lands on 60 and then on 66. Does he land on 64?\" Says Deeku is first -> \"A bigger jump does not win. Look at where each animal lands.\"\n"
        "- Hints, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Which stones does each animal land on? Look for a stone with both marks.\" (2) visual: the Lists open with both rows of multiples. (3) almost the answer: the first gold stone is ringed, for example \"Both rows have 12. 12 is the first common multiple of 3 and 4.\" In Star Challenge 4: \"Robby lands on 64. Check Deeku: 6 × 10 = 60 and 6 × 11 = 66.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: equal sizes -> \"Both jump 4, so every stone is a common multiple.\" No gold stone up to 72, for example 7 and 11 -> \"No stone up to 72 is gold. 7 × 11 = 77 is the first stone both land on.\"\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- A star challenge is done when its result is on screen (3 stones; 2 numbers; five multiples for each pair; the three answers about the food).\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"A stone both animals land on is a common multiple.\" \"The common multiples are the multiples of the first one.\" \"The list never ends.\" Think and talk: \"What do you notice about the common multiples of different pairs of numbers?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: jungle stream; grey river stones 0 to 72; cobbled road of 65 stones, carrot and mushrooms; Robby, Deeku and a frog (flat drawings); teal ring, amber diamond, gold glow and token (code-drawn). Language: English."
    ),
    "M2.S10.T10.C32.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Trail Jumper\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M2.S10.T10.C32.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity\n"
        "- Textbook source: Chapter 13 Animal Jumps, Mowgli's trail and Let Us Do questions 3 to 7 with the lines for jumps of 3, 5 and 10. Practice jump 6 and the pair 20 and 30 (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student sees that a jump of n steps from 0 lands exactly on a number when n is a factor of it, and that the jump sizes that land on every number of a list are the common factors of the list.\n"
        "- Out of scope: common multiples, prime numbers, finding factors from arrays.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- Mowgli's jungle trail: a winding path of 61 round stones numbered 0 to 60 in four loops (flat drawing). Mowgli sits on stone 0. Eleven friends sit beside their stones as in the book: ant 4, spider 9, frog 12, bird in its nest 14, Kaa the snake 21, Sher Khan the tiger 25, Baloo the bear 30, Akela the wolf 35, deer 39, rabbit 50, monkey 57. A footprint dial under the trail shows the jump size 6 (locked here), and a round green \"Go\" button sits by Mowgli.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Mowgli jumps 6 steps at a time. Which friends will he meet? Tap them.\"\n"
        "- The child taps the friends they think he will meet, then presses \"Go\".\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Trail: big stone numbers; friends two stones wide; a friend Mowgli lands on lights up with a name tag. Dial: minus, the size, plus (1 to 30).\n"
        "- Flags: a tap on a stone plants a blue flag (a target), a second tap removes it; at most 2, except where the book's numbers are flagged for the child.\n"
        "- Common factors board (from Activity 2): a wooden board under the trail; a size that lands on every flag is added in order. Goal chip at the top left of the stage.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Set the size, press \"Go\": Mowgli hops from 0, one jump every half second, leaving footprints, and stops when the next jump would pass 60. Each flag then shows a green tick if he landed on it, or a grey ring with the two stones he landed on either side if he skipped it.\n"
        "- Tap a friend to ring them as a guess; tap again to remove.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" (clears footprints, flags, board) and one that reads \"Check\", then \"Next\" when the goal chip is green.\n"
        "- A guess is never marked wrong. If it differs from the result the strip says, for example, \"You guessed the spider. Let us see where he lands.\"\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Mowgli jumps 6 steps at a time. Which friends will he meet? Tap them.\" Goal chip: \"Meet the friends\". Setup: dial locked at 6. Guess: tap friends to ring them, then \"Go\". Result: footprints on 6, 12, ..., 60; the frog and Baloo light up. Why: \"12 = 6 × 2 and 30 = 6 × 5, so jumps of 6 land on the frog and on Baloo. He jumps over the others.\" Help: guided, each landing shows its label, for example \"6 × 2 = 12\".\n"
        "- Activity 2 (independent). Goal line: \"Which jump sizes reach both 20 and 30?\" Goal chip: \"Common factors of 20 and 30\". Setup: flags on 20 and 30; dial free. Guess: tap 3, 4 or 5 for a size you think reaches both; then try sizes with \"Go\". Result: the board holds 1, 2, 5 and 10. Why: \"5 × 4 = 20 and 5 × 6 = 30, 10 × 2 = 20 and 10 × 3 = 30. A jump of 4 reaches 20 but skips 30: it lands on 28 and then 32.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal chip: \"Jumps of 2, 3, 5 and 10\". Four rounds, dial locked at 2, 3, 5, 10. The book's questions: (1) \"Mowgli's friends live along the trail on the marked places below. Which of his friends will he be able to visit, if he jumps by 2 steps starting from 0?\" (2) \"Which of his friends will he be able to meet if he jumps by 3 steps? 3 is a common factor of the numbers ___.\" (3) \"Which numbers will he touch if he jumps by 5 steps? ___. 5 is a common factor of the numbers ___.\" (4) \"Which numbers will he touch if he jumps by 10 steps? ___. 10 is a common factor of the numbers ___.\" Guess: ring the friends you expect; in rounds 3 and 4 also tap how many numbers he touches (6, 12 or 15; then 3, 6 or 10). Result: 2 meets the ant 4, frog 12, bird 14, Baloo 30, rabbit 50. 3 meets the spider 9, frog 12, Kaa 21, Baloo 30, deer 39, monkey 57. 5 touches 5, 10, ..., 60 (12 numbers) and meets Sher Khan 25, Baloo 30, Akela 35, rabbit 50. 10 touches 10, 20, ..., 60 (6 numbers) and meets Baloo 30, rabbit 50. The blanks fill from the footprints and lit friends. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line (the book's question): \"Let us find some common factors of the numbers 24 and 36. Note that all jumps in the following questions start from 0.\" Goal chip: \"24 and 36\". Setup: flags on 24 and 36. Round 1, parts (a) to (c): \"Can we jump by 2 steps at a time to reach both 24 and 36?\" then 3, then 4 steps. Guess: \"Yes\" or \"No\" before \"Go\". Result: yes each time. Round 2, parts (d) and (e): \"What other jumps can we take to reach both 24 and 36?\" and \"How many common factors can you find for 24 and 36? List them.\" Dial free. Guess: tap 4, 6 or 8 for how many. The child tries sizes until the board holds 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12. Why: \"6 × 4 = 24 and 6 × 6 = 36. 12 × 2 = 24 and 12 × 3 = 36. There are 6 common factors: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12.\" Round 3, part (f): \"What about jumping by 1 step each time to reach both 24 and 36?\" Guess \"Yes\" or \"No\"; result yes. Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal chip: \"12 and 13, then jumps of 4\". Part 1 (the book's question): \"What are the common factors of 12 and 13?\" Setup: flags on 12 and 13; dial free. Guess: tap 1, 2 or 3 for how many. Result: the board holds only 1. Why: \"12 and 13 have only 1 in common. A jump of 3 reaches 12 but then lands on 15, so it skips 13.\" Part 2 (the book's question): \"Find which of the following numbers can be reached by jumps of 4 steps? 4 is the common factor of the numbers ___.\" Setup: flags on 10, 16, 27, 36, 48; dial locked at 4. Guess: tap the flags you expect to be reached, then \"Go\". Result: 16, 36 and 48 get ticks and drop into the blank. Why: \"16 = 4 × 4, 36 = 4 × 9 and 48 = 4 × 12, but 4 skips 10 (8 then 12) and 27 (24 then 28).\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line (the book's question): \"Find the common factors of the following pairs of numbers.\" Goal chip: \"Pairs (a) to (i)\". Setup: nine pairs, one at a time, both flagged. Guess: tap 1, 2 or 3 for how many common factors. The child tries sizes until the board is full. Result: (a) 12 and 16: 1, 2, 4; (b) 8 and 12: 1, 2, 4; (c) 4 and 16: 1, 2, 4; (d) 2 and 9: 1; (e) 3 and 5: 1; (f) 12 and 15: 1, 3; (g) 20 and 5: 1, 5; (h) 9 and 21: 1, 3; (i) 6 and 27: 1, 3. Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- A jump of n steps from 0 lands on the multiples of n, so it lands on a number exactly when n goes into it: n is a factor of the number. A jump that skips a number lands on a stone each side of it.\n"
        "- A size that lands on every flag is a common factor; the biggest is not the only one, and 1 always works.\n"
        "- Rules: \"A jump of n lands on a number only if n goes into it exactly.\" \"A common factor reaches every number in the list.\" \"1 is a common factor of every pair.\"\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: a green tick on the flag, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick. Confetti only on the end screen.\n"
        "- Wrong: a red strip, a small shake and one friendly line. Retry is free.\n"
        "- Mistake: says a jump reaches a number it jumps over, for example 4 and 30 -> \"4 lands on 28 and then on 32. It jumps over 30, so it does not reach it.\" Stones 28 and 32 pulse.\n"
        "- Mistake: stops at the biggest common factor -> on Check: \"There are 2 more jump sizes that reach both.\" The empty slots pulse. Forgets 1 -> \"A jump of 1 lands on every stone. 1 is a common factor too.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: taps a skipped friend, for example the spider for jumps of 2 -> \"2 lands on 8 and then on 10. It skips 9.\"\n"
        "- Hints, 3 levels: (1) words: \"Count in jumps from 0. Does Mowgli land exactly on the number, or jump over it?\" (2) visual: faint footprints of the current size appear and every flag or friend on a footprint glows green. (3) almost the answer: for 24 and 36 \"2 × 12 = 24 and 2 × 18 = 36, so 2 reaches both. 3 × 8 = 24 and 3 × 12 = 36, so 3 does too. Now try 4, 6 and 12.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: a jump of 1 reaches every number; a jump of 30 lands only on 30 and 60; when one flag is a multiple of the other (20 and 5) the smaller number is a common factor; numbers that follow each other (12 and 13) share only 1.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- A star challenge is done when its result is on screen (four rounds; the full boards; 16, 36, 48 in the blank).\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"A jump of n lands on a number only if n goes into it exactly.\" \"A common factor reaches every number in the list.\" \"1 is a common factor of every pair of numbers.\" Think and talk: \"What do you notice about the common factors of different pairs of numbers?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: winding trail of 61 numbered stones (code-drawn); Mowgli and the eleven friends (flat drawings, as in the book); blue flags, footprints, ticks (code-drawn); wooden board. Language: English."
    ),
    "M3.S13.T13.C51.2D1": (
        "A. TOOL TITLE\n"
        "- Number Sorter\n"
        "B. TOOL INFO\n"
        "- Tool ID: M3.S13.T13.C51.2D1\n"
        "- Tool type: Concept-activity (one tester, four stations)\n"
        "- Textbook source: Chapter 13 Animal Jumps, Let Us Do questions 8 to 11. Practice values 14, 15, 20; 12, 105, 120, 18, 65, 100; 10, 20, 50, 80 (developer only).\n"
        "C. LEARNING OBJECTIVE\n"
        "- The student uses factors and multiples to test statements, find shared days, choose jump sizes and sort by 2, 5 and 10, and sees that no number is divisible by 10 only.\n"
        "- Out of scope: divisibility by numbers other than 2, 5 and 10, prime numbers, three or more animals.\n"
        "D. TOOL DESCRIPTION\n"
        "1. Opening Screen\n"
        "- A jungle pond clearing from above (flat drawing). Three overlapping circles of lily rings: pink \"Only by 2\" on the left, purple \"Only by 5\" on the right, blue \"Only by 10\" at the bottom, and \"By 2, 5 and 10\" where all three meet. At the right a wooden tester post has three grey lights marked 2, 5 and 10. Along the bottom lie three green leaf-shaped cards like the book's: 14, 15, 20.\n"
        "- Instruction line: \"Test each card. Which lights will turn green for 14?\"\n"
        "- The child taps the lights they expect, then the card 14.\n"
        "2. Scene and Objects\n"
        "- Circles take most of the stage; places where only two circles overlap are empty. Cards are thumb-wide green tags. Tester: a tapped card turns a light green if that number divides it exactly (\"20 ÷ 5 = 4, exactly\"), else the light stays grey (\"14 ÷ 5 is not exact\").\n"
        "- Statement bench (Star Challenge 1): a wooden sign with the statement, three example cards, round stamps \"True\" and \"False\".\n"
        "- Hunting days (Star Challenge 2): paw-print tiles 0 to 60 in three rows, day 0 marked \"Start\"; lights \"Sher Khan, every 3rd day\" and \"Bagheera, every 5th day\"; a tray \"They hunt together\".\n"
        "- Jump chooser (Star Challenge 3): a strip 0 to 35 with four houses (Kaa 21, Sher Khan 25, Baloo 30, Akela 35); jump stones 1 to 30; two house lights (part 1: Baloo, Sher Khan; part 2: Kaa, Akela); a basket \"Good jumps\" that takes a size only if Baloo's light is green and Sher Khan's grey, or in part 2 both green. Goal chip at the top left.\n"
        "3. Main Interaction\n"
        "- Sorting: tap lights to guess, tap a card to test it, drag a card into a circle. The drop is the guess; the tester then runs. A right drop locks in with a chime; a wrong drop slides back with one line.\n"
        "- Bench: tap \"True\" or \"False\" (the guess); the examples flip with a test line; one that breaks the statement cracks the sign. Days: tap a tile; both lights green drops a gold paw in the tray. Jump chooser: tap a stone to test it, then drag a good one into the basket.\n"
        "- Bottom buttons: \"Hint\", \"Reset\" and one that reads \"Check\", then \"Next\" when the goal chip is green.\n"
        "- A guess is never marked wrong: if it differs, the strip says, for example, \"You guessed the 10 light. Let us see.\"\n"
        "4. Activities\n"
        "- Activity 1 (guided). Goal line: \"Test each card. Which lights will turn green for 14?\" Goal chip: \"Sort 3 cards\". Setup: cards 14, 15, 20. Guess: tap the lights you expect, then the card. Result: 14 goes in \"Only by 2\", 15 in \"Only by 5\", 20 in the middle. Why: \"14 ÷ 2 = 7. 15 ÷ 5 = 3. 20 ÷ 2 = 10, 20 ÷ 5 = 4 and 20 ÷ 10 = 2: all three lights are green.\" Help: guided, the tester writes every line.\n"
        "- Activity 2 (independent). Goal line: \"Sort these cards.\" Goal chip: \"Sort 6 cards\". Setup: 12, 105, 120, 18, 65, 100. Guess: the drop. Result: 12 and 18 \"Only by 2\"; 65 and 105 \"Only by 5\"; 100 and 120 in the middle. Why: \"100 ÷ 10 = 10 and 120 ÷ 10 = 12, so both are divisible by 10, 2 and 5.\" Help: independent, tester lines only after a wrong drop.\n"
        "- Activity 3 (independent). Goal line: \"Can any of these cards stay in the circle for 10 only?\" Goal chip: \"Test the circle for 10\". Setup: cards 10, 20, 50, 80. Guess: tap \"Some\" or \"None\" for how many will stay; then drop each in \"Only by 10\". Result: every card slides back to the middle; the circle stays empty. Why: \"20 ÷ 2 = 10 and 20 ÷ 5 = 4 as well, because 10 is 2 × 5. A number divisible by 10 is divisible by 2 and 5 too.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 1. Goal line (the book's question): \"State whether the following statements are true (T) or false (F).\" Goal chip: \"Statements (a) to (f)\". Guess: \"True\" or \"False\" for each. Statements, with the three examples on the bench: (a) \"Factors of even numbers must be even.\" 8, 10, 12. (b) \"Multiples of odd numbers cannot be even.\" 3, 5, 7. (c) \"Factors of odd numbers cannot be even.\" 15, 21, 27. (d) \"One of the common multiples of two consecutive numbers is their product.\" 4 and 5, 6 and 7, 8 and 9. (e) \"The only common factor of any two consecutive numbers is 1.\" 8 and 9, 14 and 15, 20 and 21. (f) \"0 cannot be a factor of any number.\" 5, 12, 30. Result: (a) False, (b) False, (c) True, (d) True, (e) True, (f) True. Why lines: (a) \"12 is even, but its factor 3 is odd.\" (b) \"3 is odd, but 3 × 2 = 6 is even.\" (c) \"15, 21 and 27 have only odd factors.\" (d) \"4 × 5 = 20 is a multiple of 4 and of 5.\" (e) \"A jump bigger than 1 skips the next number.\" (f) \"A jump of 0 steps never leaves 0.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 2. Goal line (the book's question): \"Sher Khan, the tiger, goes hunting every 3rd day. Bagheera, the panther, goes hunting every 5th day. If both of them start on the same day, on which days will they be hunting together?\" Goal chip: \"Days they hunt together\". Guess: the first tile tapped. The child taps days until the tray holds 15, 30, 45 and 60. Why: \"They hunt together on days 15, 30, 45, 60 and so on: the common multiples of 3 and 5.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 3. Goal line (the book's question), part 1: \"In the trail shown earlier, Sher Khan's house is on number 25 and that of Baloo the bear is on number 30. Mowgli wants to meet his friend Baloo the bear but wants to avoid Sher Khan's house. How long (in steps) could each jump be?\" Goal chip: \"Meet Baloo, miss Sher Khan\". Guess: tap 5, 6 or 10 as a size that works. The basket ends with 2, 3, 6, 10, 15, 30. Why: \"These jumps land on 30 and skip 25. 1 and 5 land on 25 too.\" Part 2: \"What number of jumps (in steps) he could choose so that he can meet both Kaa, the snake, at 21 and Akela, the wolf, at 35?\" Goal chip: \"Meet Kaa and Akela\". Guess: \"Yes\" or \"No\" for a jump of 5. The basket ends with 1 and 7. Why: \"7 × 3 = 21 and 7 × 5 = 35. A jump of 3 skips 35.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "- Star Challenge 4. Goal line (the book's question): \"Sort the following numbers into those that are (a) divisible by 2 only (b) divisible by 5 only (c) divisible by 10 only (d) divisible by 2, 5, and 10.\" Goal chip: \"Sort 15 cards\". Setup: the book's cards in three rows of five: 90, 22, 38, 30, 75, 45, 66, 78, 62, 40, 84, 56, 25, 95, 55. Guess: the drop. Result: 2 only: 22, 38, 66, 78, 62, 84, 56 (7); 5 only: 75, 45, 25, 95, 55 (5); 10 only: none; 2, 5 and 10: 90, 30, 40 (3); then the child taps the empty circle. Why: \"10 is 2 × 5, so a number divisible by 10 is also divisible by 2 and 5. No number is divisible by 10 only.\" Help: independent.\n"
        "5. Mathematical Understanding\n"
        "- A number is divisible by 2, 5 or 10 when that number is a factor of it. A number divisible by both 2 and 5 is divisible by 10, so the circle for 10 only stays empty.\n"
        "- One example that breaks a statement shows it is false. Shared days are common multiples; jump sizes reaching two places are common factors.\n"
        "6. Feedback and Hints\n"
        "- Correct: the card locks in, the goal chip turns green with a gentle bounce, the pill gets a tick. Confetti only on the end screen.\n"
        "- Wrong: a red strip, a small shake, one friendly line; retry is free.\n"
        "- Mistake: a card in \"Only by 10\" -> \"A number divisible by 10 is also divisible by 2 and by 5, because 10 is 2 × 5. It goes in the middle.\" A card where two circles overlap -> \"A number divisible by 2 and by 5 is divisible by 10. It goes in the middle.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: 40 in \"Only by 2\" -> \"40 ÷ 5 = 8, exactly, so 5 divides it too. Check the 5 light.\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a wrong stamp -> a clue, for example (a) \"Is 3 a factor of 12? Is 3 even?\" (f) \"Mowgli jumps 0 steps at a time. Can he leave 0?\"\n"
        "- Mistake: a day with one light green -> \"Sher Khan hunts on day 3, but Bagheera does not.\" Day 0 -> \"Day 0 is the day they start together. When do they hunt together again?\"\n"
        "- Mistake: 5 in the basket for Sher Khan -> \"5 lands on 30, but it lands on 25 as well.\"\n"
        "- Hints, 3 levels: (1) words: \"What does divisible by 2 mean? 2 goes into the number exactly. Check 2, then 5, then 10.\" (2) visual: the tester runs on the card, tile, stone or example. (3) almost the answer: \"65 ÷ 5 = 13, exactly, so the 5 light is green. 65 ÷ 2 and 65 ÷ 10 are not exact, so 65 goes in the circle for 5 only.\"\n"
        "- Edge cases: 100 still goes in the middle.\n"
        "7. Ending\n"
        "- Each star challenge is done when its result is on screen.\n"
        "- End screen: \"Well done!\" Remember: \"A number divisible by 10 is also divisible by 2 and by 5.\" \"One example that breaks a statement shows it is false.\" \"Shared days are common multiples. Jumps that reach two places are common factors.\" Think and talk: \"Why is the circle for 10 only empty? Can you find a number that belongs there?\" Button: \"Start again\".\n"
        "8. Assets and Language\n"
        "- Images: jungle pond clearing; lily-ring circles; green leaf-shaped cards (code-drawn); wooden tester post, sign, stamps; paw-print tiles; trail strip with four houses; Mowgli, Sher Khan, Bagheera, Baloo, Kaa, Akela (flat drawings). Language: English."
    ),
}

OBJECTIVES = {
    "M1.S1.T1.C2.2D1": "The student will understand that a multiplier fits when every number that came out of the box divides by it exactly, and that more than one multiplier can fit.",
    "M1.S4.T4.C10.2D1": "The student will understand that every way of laying tiles in equal rows is a multiplication whose two numbers are factors, and that a number with only one array has just two factors.",
    "M2.S7.T7.C21.2D1": "The student will understand that the stones two jumpers both land on are common multiples, and that they are the multiples of the first one.",
    "M2.S10.T10.C32.2D1": "The student will understand that a jump of n steps lands on a number when n is a factor of it, and that the jump sizes reaching every number of a list are its common factors.",
    "M3.S13.T13.C51.2D1": "The student will understand how factors and multiples decide true or false statements, shared days and jump sizes, and why no number is divisible by 10 only.",
}
